To expand a signed \(2\)'s complement integer while preserving its value, we apply <strong>sign extension</strong> by copying the original bits into the lower positions and filling all higher-order positions with the original sign bit (MSB). For example, a \(3\)-bit negative number \(\mathbf{1}01_2\) (value \(-3\)) sign-extends to \(5\) bits as \(\mathbf{11}101_2\), which retains the exact same arithmetic value: \(-2^4(1) + 2^3(1) + 2^2(1) + 2^0(1) = -16 + 8 + 4 + 1 = -3\). <strong>Example 1: Positive Number</strong><br/>Let the \(3\)-bit signed number be \(\mathbf{0}11_2\). <ul><li><strong>Value in 3 bits:</strong><br/>\[-2^2 \cdot \mathbf{0} + 2^1 \cdot 1 + 2^0 \cdot 1 = 0 + 2 + 1 = +3\]</li><li><strong>Extended to 5 bits by repeating MSB (\(\mathbf{0}\)):</strong> \(\mathbf{00}011_2\)</li><li><strong>Value in 5 bits:</strong><br/>\[-2^4 \cdot \mathbf{0} + 2^3 \cdot \mathbf{0} + 2^2 \cdot 0 + 2^1 \cdot 1 + 2^0 \cdot 1 = 0 + 0 + 0 + 2 + 1 = +3\]</li></ul> <strong>Example 2: Negative Number</strong><br/>Let the \(3\)-bit signed number be \(\mathbf{1}01_2\). <ul><li><strong>Value in 3 bits:</strong><br/>\[-2^2 \cdot \mathbf{1} + 2^1 \cdot 0 + 2^0 \cdot 1 = -4 + 0 + 1 = -3\]</li><li><strong>Extended to 5 bits by repeating MSB (\(\mathbf{1}\)):</strong> \(\mathbf{11}101_2\)</li><li><strong>Value in 5 bits:</strong><br/>\[-2^4 \cdot \mathbf{1} + 2^3 \cdot \mathbf{1} + 2^2 \cdot 1 + 2^1 \cdot 0 + 2^0 \cdot 1\] \[= -16 + 8 + 4 + 0 + 1 = -3\]</li></ul> Since the decimal values remain identical (\(+3 \rightarrow +3\) and \(-3 \rightarrow -3\)), the proof holds true. \[\boxed{
\begin{array}{c}
\text{(C) copy the original byte to the less significant byte of the word}\\
\text{and make each bit of the more significant byte equal to the}\\
\text{most significant bit of the original byte}
\end{array}
}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li><strong>Sign Extension:</strong> This process expands the bit-width of a signed number by replicating its sign bit (MSB) into all higher-order positions, ensuring the value remains unchanged.</li></ul></div>

